module test.claim.run_verdict_exit_status_witness_test


// WHY THIS IS WET, and why wet is the only honest option.
//
// The property is: `gunbc run` maps the ProcessExit its entry returns to the PROCESS exit
// status. That is an external effect which IS the subject, so DESIGN's witness-cost rule
// admits it wet — and nothing cheaper can observe it. An in-process assertion cannot see
// the status of the process running it, and a Rust unit test is not enrolled anywhere on
// this repository (the Rust suite left CI 2026-07-11, operator ruling, local-only), so a
// Rust assertion reported as coverage would be the inert lens DESIGN calls a lie.
//
// SIZE IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN: three subprocess invocations of a trivial fixture, one per
// arm of the driver's verdict map. The smallest crossing specimen is the requirement plus
// one; a realistic-looking entry would add cost that proves nothing about the boundary.

data run_verdict_wet_subject_note: String = "Enrolled because the property was CORRECT AND UNWITNESSED and a reimplementation therefore dropped it in silence. The cli-run cut deleted handle_run_with_options and rebuilt the seam; the first version printed the returned ExitFailure and exited 0. A planted-drift control caught it — the generated-artifact gate returned ExitFailure with code 1 naming the exact drifted file, and the driver exited 0. Every consumer of `gunbc run` would have reported green regardless of content, across the drift gate, the floor and every witness invocation, while the located diagnostic scrolled past in stdout looking like information. Neither a clean run nor a malformed-argv refusal could have detected it: both are satisfiable by a seam that always exits 0 on anything that parses. These rows are the executing evidence that the arm stays real, and they do not retire when they green — per DESIGN section 4b they become the permanent regression control for the class."

data run_verdict_wall_note: String = "The measured wall of the ORIGINAL planted-drift control was 143s, because it ran the whole generated-artifact gate over roughly seventy artifacts. These rows do not: they invoke a three-function fixture whose bodies are a constructor each.

CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT 2026-08-16, and the correction is the point of this row. An earlier revision asserted 'the cost is subprocess startup plus the fixture's own resolve, not a corpus scan'. EXECUTED — claim_batch --wet, three rows, locally on this session host at a41c68c3231 plus the enrolment change, GUNBC pointed at target/release/gunbc — the walls are 48060ms, 50166ms and 56185ms. Subprocess startup plus a three-constructor resolve does not cost fifty seconds, so the claim was false as written and the fixture's smallness does not buy what it said it bought.

WHAT THE COST ACTUALLY IS: each invocation is a separate `gunbc run` process, and each pays build_both_closure_edge_index — a CORPUS-wide edge index whose cost DESIGN records as size-independent (measured there at 51.24s on a 159-module closure against 50.98s on a 504-module one, 44.96-77.11s across runs). So the dominant term is fixed per process and is not a function of this fixture at all; shrinking the fixture further would move these numbers by nothing. Three rows, three processes, three edge indices.

A STRUCTURAL FINDING ABOUT SOURCE-ROOT GRANULARITY, recorded because it is the reason the cost cannot be reduced rather than a dead end. The obvious fix for a corpus-wide per-process cost is to hand each process a narrower corpus. That option does not exist here: the fixture imports only process, so its true semantic closure is two modules — but std lives under the SAME dag root as the fixture, and a source root is a directory. There is no directory that contains dag/std/process.dag and dag/test/claim/run_verdict_exit_status_fixture.dag and not the rest of dag. So the granularity at which cost is charged (the source root, a storage fact) is coarser than the granularity at which dependency is declared (the import, a semantic fact), and a witness pays for the former while needing only the latter. Any witness invoking the seed as a subprocess pays this, not just these three. Dissolve-on: the module-identity-vs-storage lane, where a module's identity stops being its path — at which point a process can be handed a declared closure instead of a directory.

CONSEQUENCE FOR ENROLMENT, stated rather than left to be discovered: bin_witness_wet_per_row_wall_budget_seconds is 60. All three rows are UNDER it, which is the declared criterion for BinWitnessWet, so that is where they are enrolled. But 56185ms is a 6% margin, measured on one host under this session's load, and a CI runner under contention is the environment where a 6% margin is spent. The re-home precedent is explicit and cheap: excl_no_fake_anomalies_reason records rows moving to FalsifierRehomedBinWet at ~85-90s. If a measured CI run puts any of these three over 60s, the correct response is that flip — not shrinking the fixture, which cannot reach a corpus-wide fixed cost, and not raising the budget."

data run_verdict_refusal_polarity_note: String = "THE CAUSE IS A TYPE, NOT AN ASSERTION. tools.host_prelude run_witness_bin, run_witness_bin_program and run_witness_bin_ready_program all return Bool, and its run_witness_bin_program body collapses WitnessBinRefused to false — the SAME VALUE a genuine nonzero exit produces. One bit cannot carry 'ran, exit nonzero' and 'could not run' as different states, so NO assertion over that helper can separate them and no care in the assertion fixes it. That is the state-space conflation DESIGN names, with the specific consequence that the refusal path and the failure path share a value.

THE GENERAL RULE THIS ROW EXISTS UNDER: never assert == false over a call whose REFUSAL path also yields false. The polarity is what makes a row vacuous, not its wetness — the two positive rows here were fine in the same context precisely because refusal and their expected value differ.

MEASURED, not hypothetical. An earlier revision of the failure row called run_witness_bin and asserted == false. Executed through claim_batch, which runs HERMETIC, it PASSED WITHOUT EXECUTING A SUBPROCESS: hermetic mode refused IsExecutable, the refusal read as false, and the row answered yes on nothing. The two positive rows FAILED loudly in that same run ('hermetic mode: no mock_response for operation IsExecutable — refusing to fabricate Unit'), which is what kept the set honest while the individual row was not.

THE FIX IS THE UNCONFLATED ROUTE, already in the tree: the failure row now calls WitnessBin.Run directly and reads result.success from a RECORD. A hermetic context cannot satisfy it — the operation refuses and the witness fails loudly — so the row can no longer answer yes on nothing. The precedent is typed_witness_bin_run_loud_failure_red_control_holds in typed_witness_invocation, whose own note records that this route replaced a cheaper one to keep exactly this discrimination.

DISSOLVE-ON: run_witness_bin returns a result that distinguishes an unexecuted invocation from a nonzero exit, at which point rows may use the helper without the polarity hazard. Rewriting an assertion does not discharge this; changing the return type does.

THE HAZARD IS NOW STRUCTURALLY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE RATHER THAN AVOIDED BY CARE. No row calls the helper, so the import is gone — and a construction that is not imported cannot be written by a later editor who has not read this note, which is the difference DESIGN draws between construction and validation. The note is retained because the RULE is general and the receipt above is the measured evidence for it, not because this file is still exposed. Keeping the rule while removing the exposure is the intended end state, not a leftover.

STRENGTHENED 2026-08-16, AND THE DEFECT WAS INTRODUCED BY THE FIX ABOVE. Moving the refusal row onto the unconflated route left it asserting result.success == false — which is what the failure row already asserted. TWO ROWS, ONE PREDICATE, DIFFERENT SUBJECTS: one claims a DECLARED ExitFailure code reaches the process status, the other claims a value carrying no verdict is REFUSED. Both are satisfied by 'exited nonzero for any reason', so neither could tell it had exercised its own arm, and both would pass against a driver that exited 1 for everything — a fabricated plausible output one level up from the one this file exists to catch. The failure row in particular tested nothing about the CODE, which is the half that makes the mapping a mapping.

THE FIX IS TO ASSERT THE CODE, because the three arms have three distinct ones and the driver is the single authority for each: ExitSuccess -> 0, an ExitFailure -> its own declared code passing through exit_status_for, NotProcessExit -> 2. So the rows now read exit_code == 3 and exit_code == 2 against a fixture declaring 3, and the three rows answer differently rather than two answering identically.

THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT FOUND IT, worth keeping because it is cheap and general: after any substitution, for every file holding both a positive and a negative control, CHECK THAT THEY STILL ANSWER OPPOSITELY ON THE SAME PREDICATE. A negative control is correct only when its subject is wrong IN A PARTICULAR WAY; a substitution preserves the type and the shape but not the wrongness, and the wrongness is the entire content of the control. Nothing was red — a negative control that has quietly become a restatement of its positive sibling reports success."

// The failure arm. A returned ExitFailure MUST reach a nonzero status.
// This is the exact defect the planted-drift control caught; if this row ever greens by
// returning true, the driver is reporting success for a run that declared failure.
test fn run_failure_verdict_reaches_nonzero_exit() -> Bool {
  let toplevel = git.Inspect.Toplevel()
  let result = WitnessBin.Run(
    workdir: toplevel.path,
    bin_path: concat(toplevel.path, "/target/release/gunbc"),
    args: [
      "run",
      "--source-root", "dag",
      "--entry", "dag/test/claim/run_verdict_exit_status_fixture.dag",
      "--function", "verdict_failure"
    ]
  )
  result.exit_code == 3
}

// The positive control. Without it the row above is satisfiable by a seam that fails on
// everything, which would be just as wrong and far more visible.
//
// ON THE ROUTE, and it is the same route as its two siblings deliberately. An earlier
// revision called run_witness_bin, which was SOUND on polarity — its refusal yields false
// and this row expects true, so a refusal could not satisfy it. It was wrong on two other
// axes. It resolved the binary from the GUNBC env var by name while the sibling rows resolve
// an explicit bin_path under git.Inspect.Toplevel, so the three rows could invoke DIFFERENT
// BINARIES and would still all pass — a trio that is supposed to compare three arms of one
// driver must reach one driver. And it asserted a Bool, which cannot participate in the
// self-evidencing property the other two have.
test fn run_success_verdict_reaches_zero_exit() -> Bool {
  let toplevel = git.Inspect.Toplevel()
  let result = WitnessBin.Run(
    workdir: toplevel.path,
    bin_path: concat(toplevel.path, "/target/release/gunbc"),
    args: [
      "run",
      "--source-root", "dag",
      "--entry", "dag/test/claim/run_verdict_exit_status_fixture.dag",
      "--function", "verdict_success"
    ]
  )
  result.exit_code == 0
}

// A non-ProcessExit return must REFUSE rather than print-and-succeed. The host cannot
// derive a status from a value that carries none, and guessing zero is a fabricated
// plausible output.
//
// ON THE ROUTE: this row calls gunbc.WitnessBin.Run and reads result.exit_code from a
// RECORD, for the reason the polarity note above states. An earlier revision of THIS row
// asserted run_witness_bin(...) == false — the exact construction that note forbids, three
// functions below the note forbidding it. Its refusal path and its expected value are the
// same bit, so a context that cannot execute a subprocess satisfies it without executing
// one. Writing the rule is not applying it; the note was correct and the row was not.
test fn run_non_process_exit_return_refuses() -> Bool {
  let toplevel = git.Inspect.Toplevel()
  let result = WitnessBin.Run(
    workdir: toplevel.path,
    bin_path: concat(toplevel.path, "/target/release/gunbc"),
    args: [
      "run",
      "--source-root", "dag",
      "--entry", "dag/test/claim/run_verdict_exit_status_fixture.dag",
      "--function", "verdict_not_process_exit"
    ]
  )
  result.exit_code == 2
}
